#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# interval_intersection

#% text
# `interval_intersection(i1, i2, ...)` gives the intersection of intervals and centered intervals.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Real intervals give an `interval` of the pieces common to all (`interval()` when there are none).

#% item
# When a centered interval with a complex centre is involved, a common box of the complex plane is given as one centered interval; a real result is an `interval`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
interval_intersection(interval([-2, 3]), interval([1, 4]))
#% output
#= interval([1, 3])

#% cell
interval_intersection(interval([-2, 0]), interval([1, 4]))
#% output
#= interval()

#% cell
interval_intersection(interval([-2, pi]), interval([e, 4]))
#% output
#= interval([e, pi])

#% cell
interval_intersection(interval([-2, -1/2], [1/2, 2]), centered_interval(0, 1 + im))
#% output
#= interval([-1, -1/2], [1/2, 1])

#% cell
interval_intersection(centered_interval(2 + 3*im, 1 + im), centered_interval(1 + im, 2 + 2*im))
#% output
#= centered_interval(2 + 5*im/2, 1 + im/2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `interval`, `interval_union`, `interval_member_q`
